WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
Hey, welcome to SpaceX Daily, episode 47.

00:00:03.500 --> 00:00:07.180
It's July 28th, 2026, and I'm Patrick in Vancouver.

00:00:07.860 --> 00:00:09.720
Here's what's happening at SpaceX today.

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:12.780
Heat shield performance on the ship upper stage

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.080
during Flight 13 now clears a key thermal margin

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.080
for repeated orbital re-entries.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:20.980
The ship upper stage completed re-entry

00:00:20.980 --> 00:00:23.280
with its heat shield tiles remaining attached

00:00:23.280 --> 00:00:25.120
and functional through peak heating.

00:00:25.820 --> 00:00:28.040
Post-flight imagery shows the tile array

00:00:28.040 --> 00:00:30.940
survived without major loss and the stage performed

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:33.780
a controlled soft splash down in the Indian Ocean.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:37.080
This outcome directly informs tile attachment

00:00:37.080 --> 00:00:39.360
and material choices for future vehicles.

00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.200
Elon Musk disclosed that Flight 13 also included

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:46.200
an unannounced test on a specific section of the ship

00:00:46.200 --> 00:00:48.220
alongside the primary objectives.

00:00:48.680 --> 00:00:51.420
The mission carried the first on-orbit test

00:00:51.420 --> 00:00:55.360
of a Starlink V3 payload aimed at higher bandwidth and power.

00:00:56.080 --> 00:00:58.880
Remember, we covered Starship developments yesterday.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:01.980
Today's intact performance moves that forward

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.940
by lowering the risk of tile loss on the next flight.

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:07.560
With the thermal protection question

00:01:07.560 --> 00:01:09.440
now showing measurable progress,

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:12.220
regulators are simultaneously reviewing

00:01:12.220 --> 00:01:14.640
the flight paths needed to fly more often.

00:01:15.240 --> 00:01:18.920
The FAA is evaluating an updated Starship trajectory

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:21.340
and designated ocean emergency zones

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:23.160
for upcoming flights from Starbase.

00:01:24.120 --> 00:01:26.200
The review covers both ascent corridors

00:01:26.200 --> 00:01:29.940
and contingency landing areas to support higher flight rates.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.240
Updated paths would affect overflight restrictions

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.020
and public safety calculations.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:39.000
The agency has not set a timeline for a final decision.

00:01:39.640 --> 00:01:42.780
While Starship works through regulatory steps on the ground,

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.060
a direct commercial competitor is moving in orbit.

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.520
Amazon filed a new application with the FCC

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:54.500
for 5,105 satellites to expand its Kuiper constellation.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.540
The request targets direct to cell service

00:01:57.540 --> 00:01:59.740
to challenge Starlink mobile coverage.

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:03.220
The filing follows earlier Kuiper authorizations

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:06.340
and focuses on spectrum and orbital slots.

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:09.580
No launch schedule was included in the submission.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:13.580
On the launch side, routine Falcon activity continues

00:02:13.580 --> 00:02:16.300
while Starship development dominates headlines.

00:02:17.020 --> 00:02:18.880
Two Falcon 9 missions are slated

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:21.640
from Vandenberg Space Force Base this week.

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.720
Both target polar orbits

00:02:23.720 --> 00:02:25.980
and will use previously flown boosters.

00:02:26.600 --> 00:02:29.340
Ground operations are already underway at the pad.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:32.880
The schedule depends on final range and weather approvals.

00:02:33.580 --> 00:02:36.600
One of the missions will deploy additional Starlink satellites.

00:02:37.140 --> 00:02:39.120
Beyond traditional satellite deployments,

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:41.620
new on-orbit infrastructure is beginning

00:02:41.620 --> 00:02:43.740
to require its own support services.

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.340
Orbs is contracted the first inspection escort

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:49.600
for a commercial orbital data center.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:52.920
The mission will demonstrate close proximity servicing

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.200
and monitoring around an active ODC platform.

00:02:56.900 --> 00:03:00.360
No operator name or orbital parameters were disclosed.

00:03:00.640 --> 00:03:03.200
The contract marks an early step toward routine

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:05.360
on orbit infrastructure support.

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:08.140
That same class of orbital infrastructure

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:10.540
is drawing early environmental scrutiny.

00:03:11.180 --> 00:03:13.740
Worth keeping an eye on, scientists have warned

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.520
that proposed SpaceX and Blue Origin orbital data centers

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.460
could increase Earth's climate burden

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.960
through manufacturing emissions

00:03:20.960 --> 00:03:22.620
and end-of-life disposal.

00:03:23.180 --> 00:03:25.880
The analysis compares projected launch cadence

00:03:25.880 --> 00:03:29.220
and satellite mass to existing industrial sources.

00:03:29.780 --> 00:03:31.780
No quantitative mitigation plan

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.580
from either company has been released.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:37.220
Resolution would require either reduced launch mass

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.600
or verified on-orbit servicing

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.260
that extends hardware life.

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:43.360
Meanwhile, the software layer,

00:03:43.620 --> 00:03:45.740
writing on SpaceX-enabled infrastructure

00:03:45.740 --> 00:03:47.120
continues to expand.

00:03:47.960 --> 00:03:50.240
Opsera has extended its forge platform

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:51.620
into the cursor marketplace

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:54.660
to add enterprise governance and context controls

00:03:54.660 --> 00:03:56.720
for AI-assisted coding workflows.

00:03:57.500 --> 00:03:59.700
Lyatrio was named one of cursor's

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.720
first official transformation partners,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.240
focusing on enterprise-scale AI coding adoption.

00:04:05.880 --> 00:04:08.300
SpaceX's satellite and launch capabilities

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:11.140
are described as creating a controlling position

00:04:11.140 --> 00:04:14.940
in future AI infrastructure beyond terrestrial clouds.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.020
On the AI front, observers in Phoenix

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:21.680
are preparing for a visible Falcon 9 launch later this week

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:25.180
with timing dependent on the final Vandenberg window

00:04:25.180 --> 00:04:27.320
and clear evening skies expected.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:30.920
In Louisiana, state-centered Bob Henskins

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:33.140
discussed potential economic effects

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:36.640
of a proposed SpaceX facility in Vermilion Parish

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:37.900
during a public forum,

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.000
where residents raised questions

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:42.500
about job creation and infrastructure strain.

00:04:43.120 --> 00:04:45.560
Community members in Memphis continue to track

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:48.520
how the city plans to allocate $3.3 million

00:04:49.140 --> 00:04:51.260
in XAI-related tax revenue

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:53.860
with proposals including public works

00:04:53.860 --> 00:04:55.280
and education programs.

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.120
From an engineering standpoint,

00:04:57.500 --> 00:04:59.500
the ship upper stage on flight 13

00:04:59.500 --> 00:05:01.820
demonstrated that its current tile array

00:05:01.820 --> 00:05:03.680
can survive peak reentry heating

00:05:03.680 --> 00:05:05.760
while preserving attachment integrity

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:08.340
all the way to a soft ocean landing.

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:11.200
The heat shield problem reduces to balancing

00:05:11.200 --> 00:05:12.140
three constraints.

00:05:12.960 --> 00:05:15.260
Incident heat flux, material ablation

00:05:15.260 --> 00:05:16.580
or thermal conductivity

00:05:17.300 --> 00:05:19.000
and the mechanical loads transmitted

00:05:19.000 --> 00:05:20.380
through the attachment points

00:05:20.380 --> 00:05:21.960
during both ascent vibration

00:05:21.960 --> 00:05:23.940
and atmospheric deceleration.

00:05:24.460 --> 00:05:26.800
The successful outcome shows the tile

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.300
to structure interface now carries margin

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:31.660
against both thermal expansion mismatch

00:05:31.660 --> 00:05:33.580
and sheer from the plasma flow.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:36.300
That margin directly lowers the risk

00:05:36.300 --> 00:05:38.100
of tile loss on the next flight

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:40.880
which would otherwise require either thicker tiles

00:05:40.880 --> 00:05:43.440
or a redesign of the attachment geometry.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:46.900
If the same interface survives a second reentry

00:05:46.900 --> 00:05:48.120
without refurbishment,

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.060
the effective cost-per-use

00:05:50.060 --> 00:05:51.740
of the thermal protection system

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.400
drops toward the raw material

00:05:53.400 --> 00:05:55.640
and forming cost of the tiles themselves

00:05:56.320 --> 00:05:58.560
rather than the full installed assembly price.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:00.820
The next data point to watch

00:06:00.820 --> 00:06:02.660
is whether the recovered ship shows

00:06:02.660 --> 00:06:04.320
any localized tile degradation

00:06:04.320 --> 00:06:07.120
that would force a change in material formulation

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:08.480
or attachment spacing

00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:11.040
before the first attempted booster catch.

00:06:12.280 --> 00:06:15.060
Following the flight 13 data released yesterday,

00:06:15.540 --> 00:06:17.360
the intact heat shield performance

00:06:17.360 --> 00:06:19.440
moves the program one step closer

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:21.460
to answering whether the ship can achieve

00:06:21.460 --> 00:06:23.180
multiple orbital reentries

00:06:23.180 --> 00:06:24.960
with minimal ground intervention.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:28.100
The plasma environment during reentry

00:06:28.100 --> 00:06:30.360
imposes heat fluxes that can exceed

00:06:30.360 --> 00:06:33.640
several megawatts per square meter for tens of seconds.

00:06:34.280 --> 00:06:37.000
The tiles must reject or absorb that energy

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:38.820
while the underlying structure stays

00:06:38.820 --> 00:06:40.260
below its temperature limit.

00:06:40.960 --> 00:06:43.060
Attachment points must also resist

00:06:43.060 --> 00:06:44.940
the dynamic pressure and vibration

00:06:44.940 --> 00:06:47.120
that peak during the hypersonic phase.

00:06:47.860 --> 00:06:50.300
The current design appears to have closed the gap

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:52.240
between the theoretical minimum mass

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:54.740
of ceramic material needed for insulation

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.080
and the added mass required for mechanical retention

00:06:58.080 --> 00:06:59.300
and gap fillers.

00:06:59.840 --> 00:07:01.900
Closing that gap further would reduce

00:07:01.900 --> 00:07:04.680
the idiot index of the thermal protection system.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:07.020
The ratio of finished installed cost

00:07:07.020 --> 00:07:09.400
to the raw material cost of the tiles

00:07:10.200 --> 00:07:11.900
bringing it nearer to the economics

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:13.700
of simple formed ceramics

00:07:13.700 --> 00:07:16.000
rather than precision aerospace assemblies.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:19.800
Engineers will next examine whether the same tile layout

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:22.600
can tolerate the higher entry velocities planned

00:07:22.600 --> 00:07:25.340
for future missions that carry heavier payloads

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.640
or target different orbital inclinations.

00:07:28.360 --> 00:07:31.800
If the recovered hardware confirms uniform tile survival,

00:07:32.140 --> 00:07:33.740
the program gains a clearer path

00:07:33.740 --> 00:07:35.860
to testing the ship on a second flight

00:07:35.860 --> 00:07:37.220
without tile replacement.

00:07:37.800 --> 00:07:41.160
That step would validate the manufacturing repeatability

00:07:41.160 --> 00:07:43.200
of the attachment method at scale

00:07:43.200 --> 00:07:45.780
and reduce the turnaround time between flights.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.560
Any localized wear patterns observed

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.360
on the Indian ocean recovery would instead point

00:07:51.360 --> 00:07:53.720
to specific adjustments in tile thickness,

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.740
coating or spacing before the design is frozen

00:07:56.740 --> 00:07:58.460
for higher cadence operations.

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:01.680
The data also informs parallel work

00:08:01.680 --> 00:08:04.800
on the super heavy booster whose own thermal protection

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.380
needs differ because of its lower velocity

00:08:07.380 --> 00:08:08.760
at stage separation.

00:08:09.440 --> 00:08:12.120
Together, these results narrow the remaining unknowns

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:15.340
around full vehicle reuse before the first tower catch

00:08:15.340 --> 00:08:17.100
attempt on a subsequent flight.

00:08:17.940 --> 00:08:22.100
And a quick market note, SPC X is at $107.38.

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:25.480
Down 4.3%.

00:08:25.480 --> 00:08:28.260
Before we go, the next data point to watch

00:08:28.260 --> 00:08:30.960
is whether the recovered ship from flight 13

00:08:30.960 --> 00:08:33.300
shows any localized tile degradation

00:08:33.300 --> 00:08:35.720
ahead of the first attempted booster catch.

00:08:36.500 --> 00:08:38.920
And that's a wrap on today's SpaceX developments.

00:08:39.400 --> 00:08:45.840
SPC X is trading at $107.38, down 4.3%.

